<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · British Isles</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Strathclyde</h1><p class="st-meta">450 CE – 1093 CE (approx.) · capital Alt Clut (Dumbarton Rock); later Govan</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">British kingdom of the Clyde, first centred on Dumbarton Rock and later at Govan, which outlasted the other northern Brittonic realms until absorbed into Scotland.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The kingdom grew from the British people of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-clyde">Clyde</a>, descendants of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/damnonii">Damnonii</a>, after Roman influence ended. Its early king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceretic-guletic">Ceretic</a>, probably the Coroticus whom St Patrick rebuked for slave-raiding in the 5th century, ruled from Alt Clut, the fortress on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dumbarton-rock">Dumbarton Rock</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhydderch-hael">Rhydderch Hael</a>, a king of the late 6th century, appears in the lives of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/columba">Columba</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-kentigern">Kentigern</a>, the patron saint of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glasgow">Glasgow</a>. In 642 Owain son of Beli defeated and killed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domnall-brecc">Domnall Brecc</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/dal-riata">Dál Riata</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-strathcarron">Strathcarron</a>. Society rested on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> farming, and its people spoke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumbric">Cumbric</a>, a northern form of Brittonic. After 870 the royal centre moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/govan">Govan</a>, where a fine collection of carved stones survives.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 870 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> kings from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dumbarton-rock">Dumbarton</a> after a four-month siege. The renewed kingdom, often called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumbria">Cumbria</a>, expanded south into Cumbria in the 10th century. Its last known king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/owain-the-bald">Owain the Bald</a>, may have died around 1018, and it was absorbed into the kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> during the 11th century.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-strathclyde.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Strathclyde" width="320" height="316"><figcaption>Image: Hogweard · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Kingdom_of_Strathclyde_9-10th_century_en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>450 CE – 1093 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Alt Clut (Dumbarton Rock); later Govan</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Rhydderch Hael, Owain son of Beli, Owain the Bald</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Cumbric</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-kingdom">United Kingdom</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Alt Clut, Kingdom of Cumbria, Ystrad Clud</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=772&amp;focus=kingdom-of-strathclyde" aria-label="Kingdom of Strathclyde on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4570 278 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Strathclyde lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4890 534L4861 599M4890 534L4892 500"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4861 599h0M4892 500h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4890 534h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4890 534h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=772&amp;focus=kingdom-of-strathclyde">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
